question 1] Visual Studio Code (VS Code) is your code editor where you will write all your files.
1. Open your browser and go to the official website: visualstudio.com.
2. Click the big Download button for your operating system (Windows or Mac).
3. Open the downloaded file to run the installer:
	• On Windows: Accept the agreement, click Next, check the box that says "Add 'Open with Code' to Windows Explorer" (it is super helpful), and click Finish.
	• On Mac: Unzip the downloaded file and drag the Visual Studio Code application icon into your Applications folder.

question 2] Node.js is a runtime environment that lets you run JavaScript on your computer outside of a browser.
1. Go to the official website: nodejs.org.
2. Look for the version labeled LTS (Long Term Support)—always pick this one because it is the most stable and reliable version. Click the button to download it.
3. Open the downloaded installer file. Click Next through the setup wizard, accept the terms, leave all settings at their defaults, and click Install.

question 3] Extensions add extra powers to your code editor to make coding easier.
1. Open VS Code.
2. Look at the left sidebar and click on the Extensions icon (it looks like four small squares, with one square floating away).
3. Install Live Server: In the top search bar, type Live Server. Find the extension made by Ritwick Dey and click the blue Install button.
4. Install Prettier: Clear the search bar, type Prettier, find Prettier - Code formatter, and click Install. (This tool cleans up your code automatically so it looks neat).

question 4] This folder will keep all your project workspace files organized in one place.
1. Go to your computer's desktop or your documents folder.
2. Right-click in an empty space, choose New, and select Folder.
3. Name the folder exactly: web-development-practice
4. Now, drag this new folder directly into the empty window of VS Code to open it up as your active workspace.

question 5] These three files form the core foundation of a website: structure, style, and logic.
1. In VS Code, hover your mouse over the name of your folder in the left sidebar (the Explorer view).
2. Click the New File icon (it looks like a small sheet of paper with a plus sign).
3. Type index.html and hit Enter. Inside this file, type ! (an exclamation mark) and press Tab or Enter. This will instantly fill the page with a standard HTML layout.
4. Click the New File icon again, type style.css, and hit Enter.
5. Click the New File icon one last time, type script.js, and hit Enter.

question 6] Live Server fires up a local testing environment so you can view changes instantly as you type.
1. Click on your index.html file in VS Code to open it.
2. Look down at the very bottom right corner of your VS Code window. You will see a button that says Go Live. Click it.
3. Your default web browser will automatically open up a new tab displaying your blank HTML webpage. The web address bar will show an address like http://127.0.0.

question 7] This step checks that your computer recognizes Node.js and its package manager (npm).
1. Inside VS Code, go to the top menu bar and click Terminal -> New Terminal. A small window panel will slide up at the bottom of your screen.
2. Type node -v and hit Enter. It will print out a version number (like v20.x.x or v22.x.x).
3. Type npm -v and hit Enter. It will print out another version number (like 10.x.x).



